import JuliaBase.Num

/-!
# Julia's `exp` and `log`, bit for bit

Julia computes `exp` and `log` with its own table-driven kernels
(base/special/exp.jl, Tang's method with a 256-entry table; base/special/log.jl,
Tang's table-driven logarithm), not with the C library, and the two differ in
the last bit on a sizeable fraction of inputs. Wilkinson samples
`x = exp(s)` on a logarithmic grid and scores rounding errors of `f(x)`, which
change completely when `x` moves by an ulp, so the port runs Julia's kernels:
with them the actual-error integrals match Julia to all digits rather than to
`1e-5`.

The `muladd`s of `exp` are fused (as LLVM compiles them on aarch64; measured
against Julia 1.13 on 253 000 inputs: fused reproduces every result, unfused
misses 43); `log` uses explicit `fma`s and its `@horner` polynomials give the
same bits either way. Julia throws a `DomainError` for `log` of a negative
number; these return `NaN`.
-/

